  "generated_description": "<div class=\"mountain-description prose prose-stone max-w-none\">\n  <p>At 5,482 feet, <strong>Woolly Tops Mountain</strong> is a summit in Tennessee. The Woolly Tops Mountain Trail covers 10.2 miles with about 1,201 feet of elevation gain \u2014 gentle grades, but the mileage is what makes it a Strenuous outing.</p>\n  <h3>The Route</h3><p>The climbing is spread fairly evenly from start to finish rather than stacked into one steep section. From the trailhead at about 5,955 feet, the route tops out near 6,188 feet. Along the way the route passes a cliff (Eagle Rocks) near mile 3.92 \u2014 useful checkpoints for judging your pace.</p>\n  <h3>Getting There</h3><p>The trailhead is at <a href=\"https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=35.69361,-83.25723\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">35.69361, -83.25723</a> (tap for driving directions). It's roughly 40 miles west of Asheville, North Carolina. Arrive early on weekends \u2014 trailhead lots at popular peaks fill quickly, and cell service is often unreliable once you leave the highway.</p>\n  <h3>When to Go</h3><p>Late May through October is the main season. Snow and ice linger into spring and return early in fall at this latitude and elevation; in winter this becomes a different, more serious undertaking requiring traction devices and warm layers.</p>\n  <h3>Know Before You Go</h3><p>The Strenuous rating comes from the National Park Service difficulty formula, which weighs total distance against elevation gain. Most hikers should budget about 4.0 hours of moving time; add margin for breaks, photos and the descent. Download the GPX track from this page before you go \u2014 cell coverage is unreliable in the mountains and a saved route works offline in any GPS app. Conditions change: verify current access, closures and weather with the official land manager before you go. Trail data is sourced from OpenStreetMap contributors (ODbL).</p>\n  <p>Looking to link objectives or plan a weekend? Nearby summits include Porters Mountain, Horseshoe Mountain and Mount Chapman \u2014 each has its own guide on this site.</p>\n</div>",
